<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Codecov: View coverage in the editor" id="Codecov-Editor-Coverage">

    <primary-label ref="codecov"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Show or hide coverage: <shortcut>Ctrl+Shift+Alt+C</shortcut></p>
        <p>Menus: editor context menu, gutter context menu</p>
        <p>Requires: a project connected to Codecov</p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Read Codecov line coverage in the editor gutter, open line details, and show, hide or refresh coverage.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>See covered, partially covered and missed lines in the editor gutter.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>The Codecov plugin marks covered, partially covered and missed lines in the editor gutter of JetBrains IDEs, using coverage your CI uploads to Codecov.</web-summary>

    <p>
        When the project is connected to Codecov, the editor gutter shows the coverage that your CI uploaded to Codecov.
        You do not need to run tests locally.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Read the coverage gutter" id="coverage-gutter">
        <p>
            Open any source file of the project.
            Colored bars next to the line numbers show the coverage of each line:
        </p>
        <list>
            <li>Green: the line is covered.</li>
            <li>Yellow: the line is partially covered, for example when only some branches of a condition run.</li>
            <li>Red: the line is not covered.</li>
        </list>
        <p>
            The bars use the line coverage colors of the current editor color scheme.
            Lines without a bar have no coverage data in Codecov, such as comments and blank lines.
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="codecov_editor_gutter.png" alt="Covered, partially covered and missed lines in the editor gutter" -->
        <p>
            Coverage is shown by default and loads when you open a file.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Open the details of a line" id="line-popup">
        <procedure title="View the coverage of a line" id="view-line-coverage">
            <step>
                Click the coverage bar next to a line.
                A popup shows the line number and its status: <control>Covered</control>, <control>Partially Covered</control> or <control>Not Covered</control>.
                <!-- planned-image src="codecov_gutter_popup.png" alt="Coverage popup for a missed line with Open in Codecov, Generate AI Prompt and Hide Coverage links" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                Click one of the links in the popup:
                <list>
                    <li><control>Open in Codecov</control> opens the file in Codecov in your browser, scrolled to the line.</li>
                    <li><control>Generate AI Prompt</control> copies a test-writing prompt for the code to the clipboard.
                        For details, see <a href="Codecov-AI-Prompts.topic"/>.</li>
                    <li><control>Hide Coverage</control> hides coverage in all editors of the project.</li>
                </list>
            </step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Show, hide and refresh coverage" id="show-hide-coverage">
        <procedure title="Show or hide coverage" id="toggle-coverage" type="choices">
            <step>
                Press <shortcut>Ctrl+Shift+Alt+C</shortcut>.
                To see or change the shortcut on your operating system, go to <ui-path>Settings | Keymap</ui-path> and search for <code>Show Codecov Coverage</code>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Right-click in the editor and select <control>Hide Codecov Coverage</control> or <control>Show Codecov Coverage</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Right-click the editor gutter and select <control>Hide Codecov Coverage</control> or <control>Show Codecov Coverage</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <!-- planned-image src="codecov_editor_context_menu.png" alt="Editor context menu with Hide Codecov Coverage, Refresh Codecov Coverage and Upload Local Report" -->
        <p>
            The setting applies to all editors of the project.
            Coverage is shown again the next time you open the project.
            The menu items appear only when the project is connected to Codecov and a repository is detected.
        </p>

        <procedure title="Refresh coverage after a new upload" id="refresh-coverage">
            <step>
                Right-click in the editor and select <control>Refresh Codecov Coverage</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            <control>Refresh Codecov Coverage</control> appears only while coverage is shown.
            The plugin keeps coverage data for up to 5 minutes, so a refresh within that time can show the same data.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Which coverage the editor shows" id="coverage-source">
        <p>
            The plugin requests the coverage of each file from Codecov without naming a branch.
            Codecov then returns the coverage of the latest report on the repository's default branch.
            Coverage that is uploaded only for your current feature branch does not appear in the gutter.
        </p>
        <p>
            Files are matched by their path relative to the Git repository root, so the paths in your Codecov reports must match the paths in the repository.
        </p>
        <tip>
            To see the effect of your current branch, open the <a href="Codecov-Metrics.topic">Codecov Metrics dashboard</a>.
            Folder coverage in the Project tool window also uses the current branch.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

